# AM01 Web Dashboard Server Configuration
#
# Read by odo-webd.service. Only WEBD_OPTS has any effect; the user and binary
# path are literal in the unit file (systemd cannot take those from the
# environment). The binary is /usr/bin/odo-webd, running as 'miner'.
#
# Reach it at http://BOARD_IP:8080/

# THE PORT IS POSITIONAL. It is argv[1], not an option:
#
#     int port = argc > 1 ? atoi(argv[1]) : 80;
#
# so WEBD_OPTS must be the bare number and nothing else.
#
# This file previously read:
#
#     WEBD_OPTS="-p 8080 -S /run/odod/status.json"
#
# describing -p/-f/-S options that odo-webd never implemented. The failure was
# silent and quite good at hiding: atoi("-p") is 0, binding to port 0 means
# "give me any free port", so bind() SUCCEEDED, listen() succeeded, and the
# daemon logged "listening on port 0" and sat on a random ephemeral port. The
# service showed active, nothing errored, and the dashboard was simply not
# where anyone looked for it. Found 2026-08-31 by reading /proc/PID/cmdline
# after netstat showed nothing on 8080.
#
# The status file path is NOT configurable either -- it is compiled in as
# STATUS_PATH "/run/odod/status.json", matching what odo-miner writes.
WEBD_OPTS="8080"
